1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:11,922
Last episode's Excel system, today in Python. And this time on real gold data.

2
00:00:12,372 --> 00:00:18,093
The result looked good. But it hid one thing, and that is the most important part.

3
00:00:19,093 --> 00:00:24,083
Hello, this is episode eleven of the SPIDER Quant Trading series.

4
00:00:24,533 --> 00:00:30,097
Last time: five rules, an Excel sheet, and an example of thirty trades.

5
00:00:30,546 --> 00:00:35,458
Today: the same rules in code, and a full backtest on real data.

6
00:00:36,458 --> 00:00:39,619
Thirty rows work fine in Excel.

7
00:00:40,069 --> 00:00:46,338
But ten years, ten markets, and ten versions of every rule? Not by hand.

8
00:00:46,788 --> 00:00:51,621
Python does the same job in a second, and exactly the same way every time.

9
00:00:52,621 --> 00:00:59,047
We took Dukascopy gold data, from March 2024 to November 2026.

10
00:00:59,497 --> 00:01:03,938
We turned hourly candles into daily ones: 692 trading days.

11
00:01:04,387 --> 00:01:11,387
Four things per day: open, high, low, close. The same columns as in Excel.

12
00:01:12,387 --> 00:01:18,579
20-day max: close.shift(1).rolling(20).max().

13
00:01:19,029 --> 00:01:25,872
That shift(1) matters. It leaves out today's row. The Excel mistake stops right here.

14
00:01:26,323 --> 00:01:30,607
The same way: the 10-day min and the 14-day ATR.

15
00:01:31,607 --> 00:01:35,892
The signal is one line: is the close above the 20-day max?

16
00:01:36,342 --> 00:01:41,827
The answer is True or False for every day. Like the IF in Excel.

17
00:01:42,827 --> 00:01:47,582
Now a loop. Each day, check: is a trade open or not?

18
00:01:48,032 --> 00:01:54,719
No trade and a signal: buy at the next open, stop two ATR below.

19
00:01:55,169 --> 00:02:04,494
Trade open: if the day's low hits the stop, out at the stop. If the close is below the 10-day min, out at the next open.

20
00:02:05,494 --> 00:02:12,574
Each trade in R: exit minus entry, divided by entry minus stop.

21
00:02:13,024 --> 00:02:17,857
The whole code, from loading data to R, is about twelve lines.

22
00:02:18,857 --> 00:02:23,531
We ran it. Sixteen trades. Seven wins, nine losses.

23
00:02:23,982 --> 00:02:30,748
Total +9.24R. Profit factor 2.52.

24
00:02:31,198 --> 00:02:40,837
Subtract 0.1R cost per trade: +7.64R, PF 2.09.

25
00:02:41,837 --> 00:02:44,345
Now look at the trades one by one.

26
00:02:44,795 --> 00:02:54,513
One trade: entry on 29 August 2025, exit on 28 October. Alone, +8.29R.

27
00:02:54,963 --> 00:03:01,310
Remove it, and the other fifteen trades add up to only +0.95R.

28
00:03:02,310 --> 00:03:07,065
Sixteen trades are far too few. One trade changed the whole story.

29
00:03:07,515 --> 00:03:14,672
And in this period gold itself went from 2,328 to 4,061: about 74%.

30
00:03:15,122 --> 00:03:20,033
In a strong trend, breakouts always look good. Do not put money on this result.

31
00:03:21,033 --> 00:03:24,821
Next step: more years, and more markets.

32
00:03:25,271 --> 00:03:29,477
And data that was never looked at while building the system.

33
00:03:29,927 --> 00:03:34,289
Remember episode nine? This is how you avoid overfitting.

34
00:03:35,289 --> 00:03:40,279
So, today in short. The Excel rules, twelve lines in Python.

35
00:03:40,728 --> 00:03:46,920
Use shift(1) so you never peek at the future. Every trade in R, minus costs.

36
00:03:47,370 --> 00:03:54,527
And look inside the result: if a few trades earn everything, it may be luck, not edge.

37
00:03:55,527 --> 00:04:02,998
Next episode: out-of-sample and walk-forward testing. How to test a system on unseen data.

38
00:04:03,448 --> 00:04:08,358
If this was useful, subscribe and save the playlist.

39
00:04:08,809 --> 00:04:14,843
For trade setups, our Telegram and Instagram are in the description. See you in the next episode.

